	</article><article id="firehose-186354698" data-fhid="186354698" data-fhtype="story" class="fhitem fhitem-story article usermode thumbs grid_24">
		<span class="sd-info-block" style="display: none">
			<span class="sd-key-firehose-id">186354698</span>
			<span class="type">story</span>
			
		</span>







	
	

<header>
	
		<span class="topic" id="topic-186354698">
			<a href="//slashdot.org/index2.pl?fhfilter=crime" onclick="return addfhfilter('crime');">
			
				<img src="//a.fsdn.com/sd/topics/crime_64.png" width="64" height="64" alt="Crime" title="Crime">
			
		</a>
		</span>
	
    
	<h2 class="story">
		

		

		
		

		

		

		

		<span id="title-186354698" class="story-title"> <a onclick="return toggle_fh_body_wrap_return(this);"  href="//yro.slashdot.org/story/26/10/18/0323207/saudi-openai-employees-arrest-revives-fears-us-tech-companies-are-hiring-spies">Saudi OpenAI Employee's Arrest Revives Fears US Tech Companies Are Hiring Spies</a> <span class=" no extlnk"><a class="story-sourcelnk" href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/us/articles/fbi-arrest-revives-fears-u-090000730.html"  title="External link - https://www.yahoo.com/news/us/articles/fbi-arrest-revives-fears-u-090000730.html" target="_blank"> (yahoo.com) </a></span></span>

		
		
		<!--<span class="comments commentcnt-186354698" >8</span>-->
		

		
		<!-- comment bubble -->
		
			<span class="comment-bubble"><a href="//yro.slashdot.org/story/26/10/18/0323207/saudi-openai-employees-arrest-revives-fears-us-tech-companies-are-hiring-spies#comments" title="">8</a></span>
		
	</h2>
	<div class="details" id="details-186354698">
		<span class="story-details">
		<span class="story-views">
			<span class="sodify" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better"></span><span class="icon-beaker pop1 " alt="Popularity" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')"><span></span></span> 
		</span>
		</span>
		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  EditorDavid
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186354698" datetime="on Sunday October 18, 2026 @12:34AM">on Sunday October 18, 2026 @12:34AM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">spy-vs-HR</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186354698">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186354698" class="p">
			
		 	
				That Saudi national working at OpenAI and <a href="https://yro.slashdot.org/story/26/10/14/0231224/fbi-arrests-saudi-openai-employee-for-planting-recording-device-in-conference-room">arrested by the FBI</a> "has not been charged with foreign espionage," <a href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/us/articles/fbi-arrest-revives-fears-u-090000730.html">writes the Washington Post</a>, "and the U.S. government has not said whether he was acting alone or as an agent of a foreign government."<br> <br>

But "there are signs that the U.S. government is exploring the latter possibility," they add, citing comments from Chris McGuire, a member of the National Security Council during the Biden administration:

<i>The Department of Justice's press release about the incident says that the National Security Division's Counterintelligence and Export Control section, whose responsibility includes prosecuting spies, is assisting with the case...  A Washington-based Middle East expert, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to avoid incurring wrath from Saudi Arabia, said he believed the Saudi government was almost certainly involved in some way. "No one does this, not even for patriotism, without getting paid," said the person. And money would not be distributed without agreement by the Saudi royal court, he said. The Saudis lag behind their regional rival, the United Arab Emirates, in artificial intelligence development, he added.</i> <br>

Friday America's Director of National Intelligence <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/ai-espionage-openai-jay-clayton.html">told CNBC</a> that espionage, "both commercial espionage, and I would say state-sponsored espionage, are realities. It's something that our private sector companies need to be wary of."   (And CNBC adds that"An ex-CIA official, who asked not to be named because they were discussing information that is not public, told CNBC this week that the case is 'probably the tip of the iceberg.'")<br> <br>

The suspect had recently said he helped advise government agencies in Saudi Arabia on technology, <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/14/technology/openai-engineer-hidden-device-saudi-government.html">reports the New York Times</a>:
<i>In an Arabic <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u1Dq_g-d9XI">podcast interview</a> last month, Abdulrahman Alfozan, 35, a Saudi national, said he and other Saudi tech professionals in Silicon Valley had formed a group that advised Saudi organizations, including the kingdom's social insurance agency...<br> <br>

"Silicon Valley is ground zero for espionage from a variety of countries around the world," said Amy Zegart, a professor at Stanford University who specializes in national security and intelligence. She added that she was "troubled" by the OpenAI incident and expected more cases "because these companies are so important." </i> <br>

The Washington Post says the arrest "<a href="https://www.yahoo.com/news/us/articles/fbi-arrest-revives-fears-u-090000730.html">has ignited a fierce debate</a> about the potential risks of Silicon Valley's international workforce &mdash; employees who often hail from nations seeking to rival the AI superpower status of the U.S."

<i>Foreign nationals have long formed the backbone of the American tech industry, helping U.S. companies surpass their would-be international rivals...  As recently as April, Katrina Mulligan, a senior member of OpenAI's national security team, called for an "accelerated pathway to citizenship" for such workers, referring to foreign hires as a "national strategic asset" at an event on Capitol Hill. She estimated that a significant number of OpenAI's employees were not U.S. citizens, including Chinese nationals. Just half of the 300 top AI researchers in the world, she said, were American.<br> <br>

Other industry leaders &mdash; as well as some lawmakers &mdash; have long accused top-tier tech companies with many foreign workers of disadvantaging Americans and being breeding grounds for Chinese espionage. Palmer Luckey, CEO of weapons maker Anduril, and venture capitalist Shaun Maguire of Sequoia Capital took to X on Wednesday to revisit arguments that the industry should focus on hiring U.S. citizens. "Silicon Valley has operated with maximum openness and maximum naivete toward foreign tradecraft," Maguire wrote... At OpenAI on Wednesday, employees continued tense conversations about the political implications of the arrest for immigrant workers, two OpenAI employees said, speaking on the condition of anonymity to describe internal discussions...  The arrest triggered a discussion on OpenAI's internal Slack messaging platform among employees about whether the incident might increase pressure from the Trump administration on OpenAI to not hire foreign nationals, said one of the people. <br> <br>

In March, Emil Michael, the Pentagon's chief technology officer, wrote in court papers that AI developer Anthropic's reliance on staff with ties to China was among the concerns that fueled the Defense Department's conflict with the AI company.   </i><br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
	<aside class="novote">
		
	</aside>
	
	  	

		
