			<input class="tag-entry default" type="text" value="apply tags">
		</div>
		

		
		

	</menu></span>
				</div>
				
			
		</footer>
	



	</article><article id="firehose-186347794" data-fhid="186347794" data-fhtype="story" class="fhitem fhitem-story article usermode thumbs grid_24">
		<span class="sd-info-block" style="display: none">
			<span class="sd-key-firehose-id">186347794</span>
			<span class="type">story</span>
			
		</span>







	
	

<header>
	
		<span class="topic" id="topic-186347794">
			<a href="//slashdot.org/index2.pl?fhfilter=space" onclick="return addfhfilter('space');">
			
				<img src="//a.fsdn.com/sd/topics/space_64.png" width="64" height="64" alt="Space" title="Space">
			
		</a>
		</span>
	
    
	<h2 class="story">
		

		

		
		

		

		

		

		<span id="title-186347794" class="story-title"> <a onclick="return toggle_fh_body_wrap_return(this);"  href="//science.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0641232/blue-origin-announces-330m-upgrades-to-rocket-facilities-for-multiple-flights-per-week">Blue Origin Announces $330M Upgrades to Rocket Facilities for 'Multiple Flights Per Week'</a> <span class=" no extlnk"><a class="story-sourcelnk" href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/blue-origin-new-glenn-florida-expansion.html"  title="External link - https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/blue-origin-new-glenn-florida-expansion.html" target="_blank"> (cnbc.com) </a></span></span>

		
		
		<!--<span class="comments commentcnt-186347794" >12</span>-->
		

		
		<!-- comment bubble -->
		
			<span class="comment-bubble"><a href="//science.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0641232/blue-origin-announces-330m-upgrades-to-rocket-facilities-for-multiple-flights-per-week#comments" title="">12</a></span>
		
	</h2>
	<div class="details" id="details-186347794">
		<span class="story-details">
		<span class="story-views">
			<span class="sodify" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better"></span><span class="icon-beaker pop1 " alt="Popularity" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')"><span></span></span> 
		</span>
		</span>
		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  EditorDavid
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186347794" datetime="on Saturday October 17, 2026 @04:00PM">on Saturday October 17, 2026 @04:00PM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">breakfast-launch-and-dinner</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186347794">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186347794" class="p">
			
		 	
				"Jeff Bezos' Blue Origin said Friday it plans to make extensive upgrades to its Florida launch facilities," <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/16/blue-origin-new-glenn-florida-expansion.html">reports CNBC</a>, "with the goal of supporting multiple flights per week by the end of the decade."<br> <br>

"The company will add two more launch and landing pads for New Glenn, its heavy-lift, partially reusable rocket."
<i>

A $330 million expansion to its Rocket Park campus in Cape Canaveral, expected to be complete in the summer of 2027, will enable it to produce up to 150 upper stages of its New Glenn rocket annually, Blue Origin said <a href="https://www.blueorigin.com/news/building-infrastructure-for-more-frequent-launches-in-florida">in a blog post</a>.  "These investments are centered on one goal: building the capacity to support more missions, more reliably, and more efficiently," Ian Richardson, Blue Origin's senior vice president of operations, wrote in the post. "As we look ahead, this infrastructure is designed to support multiple flights per week by the end of the decade...." <br> <br>


Blue Origin said it's building a new customer integration facility with the U.S. Space Force at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station that will enable it to "prepare multiple missions at once" when it's up and running.   Blue Origin has invested more than $3 billion in its infrastructure and facilities in Florida, which now spans more than 20 sites across the state's Brevard and Orange counties, with more than 5,000 employees, [Ian Richardson, Blue Origin's senior vice president of operations, wrote in the blog post].

</i> <br>

The article notes it's part of "a flurry of investment" as SpaceX stops offering commercial rides on its Falcon 9, "with $92.5 billion invested in the commercial space sector in the first three quarters of 2026, according to <a href="https://www.spacecapital.com/reports/space-iq-q3-2026">a report this week</a> by venture capital firm Space Capital." And it happens as Blue Origin, SpaceX and other rocket providers "are seeing increasing demand from companies and governments trying to deploy satellites into orbit."<br> <br>

"Launching at a high cadence requires a connected system," <a href="https://www.blueorigin.com/news/building-infrastructure-for-more-frequent-launches-in-florida">says Blue Origin's blog post</a>, "one that manufactures vehicles at scale, prepares customer payloads efficiently, supports reliable launch operations, and quickly returns reusable boosters to service. We are building that system here in Florida," and "laying the foundation for a future where access to space is more frequent, more reliable, and more affordable, for the benefit of Earth."<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
	<aside class="novote">
		
	</aside>
	
	  	

		
