	</article><article id="firehose-186348040" data-fhid="186348040" data-fhtype="story" class="fhitem fhitem-story article usermode thumbs grid_24">
		<span class="sd-info-block" style="display: none">
			<span class="sd-key-firehose-id">186348040</span>
			<span class="type">story</span>
			
		</span>







	
	

<header>
	
		<span class="topic" id="topic-186348040">
			<a href="//slashdot.org/index2.pl?fhfilter=ai" onclick="return addfhfilter('ai');">
			
				<img src="//a.fsdn.com/sd/topics/ai_64.png" width="64" height="64" alt="AI" title="AI">
			
		</a>
		</span>
	
    
	<h2 class="story">
		

		

		
		

		

		

		

		<span id="title-186348040" class="story-title"> <a onclick="return toggle_fh_body_wrap_return(this);"  href="//linux.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0723207/linux-maintainers-can-now-submit-ai-generated-code-without-declaring-it">Linux Maintainers Can Now Submit AI-Generated Code Without Declaring It</a> <span class=" no extlnk"><a class="story-sourcelnk" href="https://www.xda-developers.com/linus-calls-banning-ai-generated-code-utterly-stupid-as-linux-submissions-no-longer-need-llm-disclosure/"  title="External link - https://www.xda-developers.com/linus-calls-banning-ai-generated-code-utterly-stupid-as-linux-submissions-no-longer-need-llm-disclosure/" target="_blank"> (xda-developers.com) </a></span></span>

		
		
		<!--<span class="comments commentcnt-186348040" >57</span>-->
		

		
		<!-- comment bubble -->
		
			<span class="comment-bubble"><a href="//linux.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0723207/linux-maintainers-can-now-submit-ai-generated-code-without-declaring-it#comments" title="">57</a></span>
		
	</h2>
	<div class="details" id="details-186348040">
		<span class="story-details">
		<span class="story-views">
			<span class="sodify" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better"></span><span class="icon-beaker pop1 " alt="Popularity" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')"><span></span></span> 
		</span>
		</span>
		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  EditorDavid
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186348040" datetime="on Saturday October 17, 2026 @03:00PM">on Saturday October 17, 2026 @03:00PM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">pared-programming</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186348040">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186348040" class="p">
			
		 	
				Linux maintainers "<a href="https://www.xda-developers.com/linus-calls-banning-ai-generated-code-utterly-stupid-as-linux-submissions-no-longer-need-llm-disclosure/">have ruled that people no longer have to declare their LLM usage</a>," reports <em>XDA Developers</em>, "as Torvalds himself calls a ban on AI 'utterly stupid.'"

<i>The first part of this story comes via a message <a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261009083137.3-1-sashal@kernel.org/">sent to the Linux mailing list</a>. It announces one of the major changes decided during the Maintainers Summit on October 8, 2026, in Prague, Czechia, where submissions to the kernel no longer need to use the "Assisted-by" tag... meaning that people can submit AI-generated code without needing to declare it as such. <br> <br>

In some correspondence regarding concern about this new disclosure removal, the creator of Linux, Linus Torvalds, <a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/CAHk-=whTs09_ozN+e09GU68N1wPP56MUzDjrg9rnKxQUa0A_4Q@mail.gmail.com/">made his thoughts known</a>...  [Torvalds] compares an AI ban to getting rid of text editors, as doing so would prove that someone could write a good commit message without making any mistakes.   He then rounds off his argument by asking, "Do you not see how UTTERLY STUPID those kinds of rules would be?" and stating that he simply expects the people working on the Linux kernel to "understand what they are doing."</i> <br>

Here's the last line of Torvalds' post.  "Quite honestly, in my experience, the people I respect and call intelligent are not the ones that shy away from using new and interesting tools."<br> <br>

In a related story, Torvalds says he's used AI <a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linus-Torvalds-Assoc-Array">to help him understand patches</a> that were hard to follow. And this week Torvalds even posted an LLM-assisted patch, <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/software/linux/linus-torvalds-posts-his-first-llm-assisted-patch-to-the-linux-kernel-after-issuing-clear-pro-ai-directives-the-bossman-makes-use-of-ai-tooling-himself">reports <em>Tom's Hardware</em></a>:

<i>The development branch purports to fix an old issue with massive cache contention when many CPUs are reading the same file &mdash; a scenario potentially encountered when using shared libraries, databases, and web servers. Several developers had already made attempts over the years at fixing the issue, all with varied results, none quite ideal as an all-encompassing solution... He's now posted his solution <a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linus-Torvalds-Claude-Code">in a development branch</a>, and claims that he's seen a 23x performance improvement in a dedicated synthetic benchmark, though he notes that actual real-world impact will be far less significant, as "no real load" will witness those figures.  </i> <br>

Torvalds used Anthropic's Claude Code, <a href="https://thenewstack.io/torvalds-claude-code-kernel/">reports The New Stack</a>, and "said the assistant wrote the implementation, tests, and initial documentation, and reviewed the code under his direction. He ran the benchmarks himself, despite having previously <a href="https://thenewstack.io/torvalds-ai-programming-productivity/">criticized developers</a> for claiming AI writes 99% of their code."  Tuesday Torvalds <a href="https://lkml.iu.edu/2610.1/13146.html">submitted a 10-patch series</a>.<br> <br>

"Reviewers have since identified a race condition and raised concerns about memory reclamation," adds <em>The New Stack</em>.  "Torvalds has handed further development to Kiryl Shutsemau, a Linux kernel developer who previously worked on the same bottleneck."<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
	<aside class="novote">
		
	</aside>
	
	  	

		
