		<span class="sd-info-block" style="display: none">
			<span class="sd-key-firehose-id">186343690</span>
			<span class="type">story</span>
			
		</span>







	
	

<header>
	
		<span class="topic" id="topic-186343690">
			<a href="//slashdot.org/index2.pl?fhfilter=space" onclick="return addfhfilter('space');">
			
				<img src="//a.fsdn.com/sd/topics/space_64.png" width="64" height="64" alt="Space" title="Space">
			
		</a>
		</span>
	
    
	<h2 class="story">
		

		

		
		

		

		

		

		<span id="title-186343690" class="story-title"> <a onclick="return toggle_fh_body_wrap_return(this);"  href="//science.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0011226/astronomers-discover-the-first-green-star">Astronomers Discover the First Green Star</a> <span class=" no extlnk"><a class="story-sourcelnk" href="https://www.space.com/astronomy/stars/astronomers-discover-the-1st-ever-green-star-a-cosmic-zombie-just-in-time-for-halloween"  title="External link - https://www.space.com/astronomy/stars/astronomers-discover-the-1st-ever-green-star-a-cosmic-zombie-just-in-time-for-halloween" target="_blank"> (space.com) </a></span></span>

		
		
		<!--<span class="comments commentcnt-186343690" >30</span>-->
		

		
		<!-- comment bubble -->
		
			<span class="comment-bubble"><a href="//science.slashdot.org/story/26/10/17/0011226/astronomers-discover-the-first-green-star#comments" title="">30</a></span>
		
	</h2>
	<div class="details" id="details-186343690">
		<span class="story-details">
		<span class="story-views">
			<span class="sodify" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better"></span><span class="icon-beaker pop1 " alt="Popularity" title="Filter Firehose to entries rated red or better" onclick="firehose_set_options('color', 'red')"><span></span></span> 
		</span>
		</span>
		<span class="story-byline">
	
				
			Posted
				by 
		
		
			
				  <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/beauhd/" rel="nofollow">BeauHD</a>
			
		
		

		
		
		<time id="fhtime-186343690" datetime="on Saturday October 17, 2026 @03:00AM">on Saturday October 17, 2026 @03:00AM</time>
		
		
			 from the <span class="dept-text">hide-and-seek</span> dept.
		
		</span>
	</div>
</header>

<div class="body" id="fhbody-186343690">
	

	
		
		<div id="text-186343690" class="p">
			
		 	
				Astronomers have identified what <a href="https://www.space.com/astronomy/stars/astronomers-discover-the-1st-ever-green-star-a-cosmic-zombie-just-in-time-for-halloween">may be the first genuinely green star</a>, a white dwarf called WD J1330+6435 located about 326 light-years from Earth. Space.com reports: <i> The researchers found that some dead star white dwarfs can go a little green around the gills after snacking on orbiting planets. An example of this is WD J1330+6435, a cool white dwarf located around 326 light-years away, which has been accreting rocky material from a planetary companion. This has enriched its atmosphere with calcium, lithium, and sodium, and the team's model suggests this would have led WD J1330+6435 to adopt a green hue.
<br> <br>
[...] The researchers think that no other star, living or dead, has ever been credibly reported as green. That makes WD J1330+6435 the first known genuinely green star. "The traces of sodium in the atmosphere of this white dwarf absorb the orange part of the spectrum, leaving behind a ghostly green glow," Hollands said. "Imagine standing on a planet and looking up at a green star. It feels like something out of science fiction." </i> The researchers also discovered what may be the reddest star ever observed, the white dwarf WD J1011+2845, located just 48 light-years away.
<br> <br>
The findings have been <a href="https://academic.oup.com/rasti/article/doi/10.1093/rasti/rzag069">published in the journal RAS Techniques and Instruments</a>.<br>
		 	
		</div>

		

		

		
	</div>
